# KNOWN GAP — the STRICTLY SIMPLER repro for `t0709`. Same
# `AddressSanitizer: stack-buffer-overflow` in `__interceptor_memcpy` <-
# `gorget_array_push`, with the HELPER ESCAPE, the `mk()` payload and the
# `.get(0).unwrap()` read ALL REMOVED. It crashes at the PUSH, in `main`.
#
# ⚠ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: `t0709`'s discriminator is OVER-SPECIFIED. It is
# filed as "`Vector[Box[Trait]]` built with `Box.new` + `push` and RETURNED
# FROM A HELPER", which reads as if the helper escape were part of the
# mechanism. It is not. A fix scoped to the helper-escape shape would miss
# this program.
#
# ⚠ THE PUSHED VALUE MUST BE A TEMP — a sharper discriminator than `t0709`
# carries, measured while writing this file. Binding the box to a named local
# first (`Box[Speaker] b = Box.new(Robot("R2"))` then `v.push(b)`) does NOT
# crash: it is rc 1 with a 35 B / 2 LEAK at pristine `f3feea79`. Only the
# TEMP form overflows. So the named-local rescue that governs so much of this
# tree's ownership behaviour ALSO separates these two failure modes, and the
# eventual fix must be checked against both spellings.
#
# This is `t0709`'s EVIDENCE, not a second filing.
#
# RED-VERIFIED at pristine `f3feea79`: ASan stack-buffer-overflow, 3/3 runs;
# unchanged by R47 Track B (a different class — drop registration, not the
# array push's element copy), 3/3 runs.
#
# INTENDED: prints `R2!`, rc 0, on C and LLVM, ASan-clean.
from std.collections import Box
from std.collections import Vector

trait Speaker:
    String speak(self)

struct Robot:
    String id

equip Robot with Speaker:
    String speak(self):
        return self.id + "!"

void main():
    Vector[Box[Speaker]] v = Vector[Box[Speaker]]()
    v.push(Box.new(Robot("R2")))
    print(v[0].speak())
